In article <C9vHyH.DJ4@cs.uiuc.edu> kadie@cs.uiuc.edu (Carl M Kadie) writes:
>I would like to see the 'hostile environment' rules defended in this
>discussion because I'd like to know if they are defensible.

	It would be wrong for anyone to say there is nothing at all, in
law or theory, in their corner. Of course they are defensible, through
those lines of argument that justify all censorship: "These ideas are so
dangerous to society, they must be outlawed, and anyone who manifests
them punished". It's a seductive idea to outlaw all evil and harm. The
task of civil-libertarians is to keep pointing out how this leads to
widespread repression, and usually comes back to work against all but
the very elite. One person's pleasure is another's perversion.

> ... All my suggestions took as a premise that the University's general SH
> policy was valid (perhaps a bad assumption). ...

	Indeed (bad). This a key, and herein lie several problems. If
the general policy is subject to capricious enforcement and causes
massive chilling effects, anything linked to it does not respect free
expression rights. Note out on the electronic frontier, First
Amendement rights are not yet as firmly established as they need to be.
So the defenses against content controls by institutions are much weaker.

--
