Note that the original recommendation refers only to "scantily clad"
females.  Somehow, this has mutated into "nude" over the course of this
discussion.  (The heat of the flames burned their clothes off?)

morgan@engr.uky.edu (Wes Morgan) writes:
>... What about this (also realistic) scenario:
>	Barbara goes to the computer lab.  As is her habit, she looks
>	at the screens of other students as she walks to her seat.  She
>	sees an image which makes her uncomfortable.
>Does some person (who may not even have noticed Barbara walking in) de-
>serve to pay a penalty for Barbara's snooping?  I think not.

Well, everybody seems to be missing one of the key points here, so I'll
foolishly stick my head up out of my hole in the ground ("Fore!"
*whack*) and point it out with a couple of analogies:

	A, B, C, and D, all of whom happen to be male, are hired as
	salespersons for a small company, and are given adjoining desks
	in a common office space.  They immediately customize their
	surroundings with pictures and mementos that make them
	comfortable: A hangs his baseball cap collection on an old
	coatrack, B arranges his seventeen rubber ducks on a foam
	pedestal, and C and D tape Playboy centerfolds to the walls next
	to their desks.  Some time afterwards, E, a female, is hired to
	augment the sales force.  She cannot help but notice C and D's
	wallpaper.  It creates an environment in which she feels
	uncomfortable.

I, for one, can relate to such an environment; my high school had a
small clique of white students who enjoyed taunting and baiting Asians,
and as I was and am fairly skinny, I was a favorite target.  Sometimes
I'd come into class on book-reading day and find hand-made racist
cartoons taped onto a corner of the wall in the back of the room.  I
couldn't help but notice them, and they sure bugged the hell out of me.

I believe that the sort of situation described in my scenario above has
already been established to constitute sexual harassment in workplaces,
although maybe C and D have to refuse to take their centerfolds down to
turn it into harassment.  The bottom line is that C and D are treading
on *very* thin ice here, and are within a few huffy "No"s and "So what
are you gonna do about it?"s from getting books thrown at them.  Also,
businesses are required by law to prominently display the text of SH and
other federal regulations where everybody can see them, sort of like
restaurants must display their licenses to serve food.  Most companies
that I know of put these regs on bboards in the copier/mail room.

Now, here come the analogies:

	F arrives to work in a CCSO computer lab, sits down in front of
	a NeXT box, places a small crystal pyramid on the monitor to
	harmonize its EMR with the One Universal Wave, and proceeds to
	read news.

	G arrives in a computer lab, tapes a favorite Playboy centerfold
	to the partition of the cubicle wall next to the monitor, and
	begins working on a programming assignment.

	H arrives in a computer lab, logs in, loads (either manually, or
	automatically in his .xinitrc file) a digitized image of a
	favorite Playboy centerfold onto the background of his monitor,
	and resumes working on a thesis.

Would anybody like to argue that G *isn't* in hot water?  Why not?  What
makes G's action substantially different from C's or D's?  Suppose
Barbara asks G to take the centerfold down; can G refuse?  If so, on
what grounds?

How is G's action different from F's, other than the fact that most of
us are merely amused by small crystal pyramids?

Finally, how is H's action substantially different from G's?  (Note that
I distinguish between text, which are comprehensible only if one snoops
over the user's shoulder and actively reads it, and a background GIF on
a 20" screen, which is visible from several meters away to a casual (and
passive) sweep of the eyes.  If H's background GIF is partially covered
by foreground windows, imagine that G's centerfold is partially covered
by similarly-arranged Post-it notes.)

Note that I am completely skirting the issues of whether Barbara is
being reasonable by feeling uncomfortable, whether nudie GIFs inherently
constitute SH, whether SH policies themselves are reasonable in ends and
means, whether our particular proposal for an SH policy is or is not
overly draconian, or whether this whole PC thing is a good trend towards
mutual, egalitarian compromise or an evil, insidious plot towards mind
control by a chosen few.  I am merely making the observation that it has
already been established, to the best of my knowledge, that centerfolds
are not appropriate for display in public workplaces, and asking for a
precise distinction between centerfolds and centerfold GIFs.

Another scenario, slightly different: suppose J (a female) brings a new
copy of Playgirl to a local restaurant, and reads it while enjoying
dinner.  Would the diners at a nearby table have a right to complain?
Would the restaurant itself (say, a male waiter)?  How is this different
from the scenarios given earlier?  I've worked out my own answer; I'd
like to hear some other persons' opinions.

Eric Wang
wang@sml0.ge.uiuc.edu

