Here is the text of the article in the "Weekend Australian", June 26-27,
which outlines the nature of the inquiry happening at UNSW at the moment.



==========================================================================


	UNI TO PROBE HARASSMENT BY ELECTRONIC MAIL

		   By Fiona Harari


The University of NSW has launched what is believed to be Australia's
first investigation into the implications of electronic mail following a
spate of claims of sexual, racial and defamatory abuse during the past
six months.

A working party convened by the head of the university's school of
computer science, Professor John Hiller, is examining the social
implications of electronic mail, which is quickly becoming a favoured
means of workplace communication for tens of thousands of Australians.

Professor Hiller yesterday refused to reveal details of the alleged
abuse and said he had been told by the university not to comment on whether
any of the matters involved legal action.

Most of the complaints, involving both teachers and students and
covering allegedly discriminatory and defamatory material, have been
handled by the university's equal employment opportunity unit, which is
attached to the vice-chancellor's office.

Professor Hiller would not reveal the number of cases that had prompted
the investigation but said that he personally had examined "less than
10" since the first complaint was made late last year.

About 23 000 of the university's students and staff are connected to
electronic mail.

With no precedents to guide the working party, the implications of its
inquiry are likely to be wideranging, as all Australian universities,
government departments and most businesses also have access to the
facility.

Anyone connected can be accessed simply by knowing their electronic mail
address.

"It is the ease of the possibility of disseminating large amounts of
material to large numbers of people that is the concern," an
institutional sociologist and member of the working party, Dr Diana
Olsberg, said.

"This is an area where there has been very little work done. People are
not aware of the implications of what might seem to them to be chance
remarks. And they are not aware of their own culpability."

Problems already identified include the alleged use of other people's
log-ons to send abusive messages.

Professor Hiller said this meant that not only was the wrong person
blamed but he or she could potentially be legally liable for those
statements.

There are also potential problems of defamation.

Messages are regularly sent to lists of people increasing the
possibility that some comments - by being sent in written form to large
numbers of people - will be construed as defamatory.

"If you write to a list, you are effectively publishing (that message).
And publishing normally has some sort of checks on it. But there are no
checks on this," he said.

But it is the medium, as much as the content of messsages that is
causing concern.

"This is a much more spontaneous medium of communication of
communication and people tend to write as they speak," Dr Olsberg said.

"I would call it digitized writing. It is a combination between speech
and writing because people tend not to redraft and consider their
implications."

Unlike the telephone where there is still some sense of human connection
because the person's voice is audible, messages sent via computer are
essentially devoid of human contact.

"People are not as cognisant of the usual courtesies and inter-personal
demands which would be obvious in face-to-face communication," Dr
Olsberg.

There are also business concerns, with fears that material moved around
electronically might not comply with copyright laws.

"There are a whole lot of laws to cope with paper and the abuse of
things on paper. And so those laws have been passed on to cope with
electronic systems," Professor Hiller said.

"I believe they are not quite sufficient. It may be that the laws are
okay but the ability to generate the problems are different."

While the working party's inquiry is ongoing, it has already spawned a
sociological study on the effects of electronic mail on social relations
in the workplace.

At the same time, a code of behaviour now has to be signed by anyone
gaining access to the university's system, in which they promise to act
respectfully and not use abusive language in the process.

Dr Olsberg said the inquiry aimed to make people behave more
considerately and responsibly towards colleagues.

"Problems have arisen which we never even thought about," she said.

"And most organizations in Australia didn't consider these things but
they are the organisational issues of the future."




==========================================================================



