wang@sparc0a.cs.uiuc.edu (Eric Wang) writes:
>I distinguish between text, which are comprehensible only if one snoops
>over the user's shoulder and actively reads it, and a background GIF on
>a 20" screen, which is visible from several meters away to a casual (and
>passive) sweep of the eyes.  

If I place a text screen in 50 point letters that says "PENIS" on it,
does this count as text or as a background GIF?   What if I'm reading a letter
in 50 point letters?  

Would you ban magazines with pictures of naked people and/or 
magazines with text about sex in them?
You seem to be implying that it's ok to ban images, and text is not
an image unless you can see it from several meters away.  How many
meters away is ok exactly?  If I'm playing stip poker on my PC in my
office but you can see it through a telescope from your dorm room, am
I offending you? 	

>I am merely making the observation that it has
>already been established, to the best of my knowledge, that centerfolds
>are not appropriate for display in public workplaces, and asking for a
>precise distinction between centerfolds and centerfold GIFs.

Inappropriate or impolite, sure.  That's the problem with this whole
debate.  Nobody has said it's nice to show centerfolds or racist
cartoons in public.  But it's not wrong or immoral or illegal.  In
fact, it is a person's right to do this.  
Along with the right to view centerfolds in public comes the right to
read the bible in public, to look at books on art in public, and to
read medical anatomy books in public, to read books about communism
in public.  You get the general gist here.
A policy that bans certain offensive things, is really just the beginning
of banning anything.

>Another scenario, slightly different: suppose J (a female) brings a new
>copy of Playgirl to a local restaurant, and reads it while enjoying
>dinner.  Would the diners at a nearby table have a right to complain?

Sure they could complain.  The right that allows you to say that 
God is great, or US policy in Iraq sucks, or I hate Bill Clinton, gives
you the right to say "That woman reading Playgirl annoys me".
It doesn't give you the right to walk over to her and bodily throw her
out on the street.  

>Would the restaurant itself (say, a male waiter)?  

Again, he could complain.  Lovely ammendment, that.  He can't go over
and throw wine in her face because he's offended.  

Your real question is, can somebody complain and have the person
stopped?  I don't think so.  Unless they're infringing on their rights, 
they can't be stopped.  (Which is one reason the bible kids keep on 
coming out to the quad)

We live in a country that allows us to do almost anything we want.
Because of this, we have opportunities other people never dreamed of.
We also get a certain degree of slime.  You have to cope with the
slime so that you can get great works of literature, plays, or art.

If you can't cope with the slime, there are plenty of countries that
opress the right to read what you want and say what you want that you
can move to.

You don't have to worry about seeing any nudie Gifs in Iran.

