>Hogwash.  I would define "common sense" in relation to file access as
>that a file is "ok" to access if you do so accordingly to it's
>system/user set protections.  Which means that if a file is world
>read, then anyone who can log onto that system has the "right" to read
>it.  Hacking into protected files, however, is not a "right."  
>
>Protections are there for a REASON.  If a user/system doesn't want a
>file to be accessed, you set the protection for owner only.  If you do
>so otherwise, it is an invite to others to use/manipulate it as the
>protections allow, barring any specific systemwide policy that is
>cleary stated every time you log on.  

I would have to agree with Amy completely, except for the part about a stated
systemwide policy.  This type of policy can be so vague that it is useless, and
if the time is spent to create a long policy, it will never be read.  It still
comes down to the permissions, if the permissions are set for others to access
files, how can you say you didn't want them to have access especially when they
have the ability to limit the access.

Now, if we believe in and follow the above reasoning, what will happen in the
following scenario:

	Joe user can look at any files on his system which have read
	permissions set to world readable.  For example /etc/passwd.
	Everyone accepts this.  What will happen when Joe uses TFTP
	to access another machine on his site, or at another site?
	If TFTP is enabled and he is able to access the other sites 
	/etc/passwd file should this be dealt with differently?
	I don't think so.  

If the site expects any level of security/privacy they should take steps to
improve security and ensure privacy.  The ability is there to limit access
to these files and other private files so how can the site expect 
astronomically high levels of security? It should be noted that there are a 
number of sites which have TFTP enabled and can have their /etc/passwd copied 
by any outsider, some of these sites haven't shadow'd their password files 
either.  In my opinion they are asking for trouble.

--
Blaine

