The entire crux of the defamation issues can be brought down to a
single point: `how do you prove that what someone typed is what
you receive?'. Simple - you can't. At _any_ stage of a message's
life it can be untraceably modified by any person along the way -
unless you use a trusted signature technique such as PGP - and even
that can possibly be bypassed (and if you post something even
slightly libellous - are you going to sign it?)

There are many factors to consider here, and after recently hearing
of a couple of incidents of alleged defamation & quick retractions
by the original poster makes you want to wonder - who stands to gain?
The lawyers invovled, because we know that in cases like these, it
will be a long time before it would be settled.

Anyway, back to the point. If, for some reason, I was alleged to have
defamed someone - I'd expect the legal entities involved to prove
beyond a doubt that a) I typed it, and b) what I typed was what the
defamee received. And I doubt their abilities in resolving those
issues quickly.

