Sure they don't. IBM PC architecture reserved memory between 640k and 1 meg
for its internal use - screen buffers and such, nothing to do with PC/DOS or
MS DOS (or DR-DOS for that matter).
At the time when IBM PC was designed (1980), micros typically had 8 to 64k
and the designers thought 640k to be real generous. They also foresaw the
advent of protected mode systems running multiple DOS box and protected
mode applications which would not care about 640k limit. So, nobody gets
the blame. Only thanks for making PC industry what it is today.

Obviously, 640kb limit does not help explaining Gregory Steshenko's case.

