In article <1993Jul12.181203.12182@Csli.Stanford.EDU> manin@Csli.Stanford.EDU (Dmitri Manin) writes:

> Like it or not, Microsoft has been and is setting standards. Besides
> Windows there are Word and Excel, to name just a couple.

There are some standards that would better not be standards, e.g.
standards that require crippling the address space to 20 bits to
retain compatibility with equipment that has been obsolete for ten
years by now. It's like claiming binary compatibility to OS/360 now
just because "there are so many of them".

I don't question the value of Word and Excel, and Microsoft used to
make some fine compilers too. But these are at least not brain-dead
standards. :-)

> As for 640
> kb, it's because of Intel/PC architecture (correct me, the
> knowledgeable ones, if I'm wrong), so microsoft has nothing to do with
> it. 

You're half-wrong - it's because of the original IBM 8088 PC
architecture.  Some half-compatibles (like the ill-fated Siemens 80186
PC-D) have had a full megabyte, the whole address space of the
86/88/186, available to DOS at a time when everybody thought DOS was
only capable of managing 640k. The limit is actually 1M, of which a
large portion is not generally usable because of a deliberate design
decision made at IBM ca. 1981 - they reserved the upper 384k for I/O
and add-on (e.g. the ROM of special controllers) space.

But this address space concept was obsoleted as early as 1983, with
the first 286 AT model. This was capable of handling more than 1
megabyte - but only if you give up binary compatibility with
1983-standard MS-DOS (or CP/M 86, for that matter :-) The main fault
of the software industry, with MS leading, has been to ignore the
286-up protected mode because of laziness to rewrite a portion of
their assembly code user software [who said "Flight Simulator"? :-)]

Now, we have the absurd situation that every computer sold now has at
least 2 megs of memory but the OS bundled with it is not capable of
managing it. If the software includes Windoze it is at least not
capable of managing it *efficiently*, for the reason that it has to
retain a certain amount of 1983-obsoleteness compatibility.

Without the intention of starting another religious war, let me just
state that from my experience the same software runs faster under
Linux than under DOS/Windoze and the former is clearly capable of
managing as much memory as you can physically stuff into a 386-based
machine, so it's my choice for appropriateness reasons. 

You see that the 640k limit is not enforced by hardware. There is a
hardware limit (also by IBM design, not the CPU) for DMA at 16M on
most mainboards. But with the right software, this can be handled
without too much hassle.

> Moreover, how is it related to the Steshenko case? If Microsoft isn't
> worth working for it, what's the fuss about?

It is absolutely not related to Steshenko in any way. :-) 
