Correct in essence, but you have the wrong culprit.  Steshenko posted
Ukrainian-baiting messages on his own time, but using an account provided
him by his employer.  Following a pair of complaints, Steshenko was
requested to quit using the company account.  He immediately did so.  He
was fired anyway.  Interval between first complaint and dismissal: eight days.

I did not find Steshenko's postings in good taste.  However, Microsoft's
firing of an employee for political views expressed on his own time, and
ultimately from an account unconnected with Microsoft, I cannot help but
regard as odious and as setting a dangerous precedent.  It rather
resembles firing someone from a job because of a letter to the editor that
included a job title.

The "freedom of speech" issue is rather complex.  On the one hand, the
only constitutional restriction is that the government may not use
repressive tactics to inhibit speech.  On the other hand, using his
posting as the grounds for dismissal (which Microsoft has now rather
weakly denied) is very likely a violation of various employment laws,
which are intended to insure that one can only be fired for things listed
in the job description--this is the same issue as the much more
politically explosive issue in which an executive fires a secretary for
not sleeping with him.

Microsoft now claims that Steshenko "seriously edited" a performance
review document.  Steshenko denies it.  Believe who you want.  But if
Microsoft fired Steshenko for his postings, then yes, the issue is cut and
dried: they had no right to do so.

