This thread is scarily coming close to describing what has been happening
this past week with our listserv list/newsgroup "bit.listserv.hellas",
which is a discussion list, in Greek, of all Greek interests: politics,
sports, music, poetry, etc.  To those of us actively involved/subscribed,
it has become more of a family than anything, being as we hold semi-annual
get-togethers, local parties, and what-have-you.  The majority of the
"members" of the list know each other personally, and if you can believe
it, the five-year history of the "List" (as we call it) has even seen a 
marriage and another engagement, too!  It is a godsend for those of us
(like me) who have little other interaction with our fellow Greeks, and even
more so for the students actually from Greece, here for graduate study.

The past month one of our main topics of conversation, per se, was 
over the moderation, restrictions, and general lack of privacy on a 
machine at the Democritus National Research Center in Athens.  The 
system administrator, Dr. Vassilis Georgiou, went so far as to close,
without warning, the accounts of approximately 5 or 6 dial-up users
for the sole reason that they were part of the "Hellas List" and 
supported the complaints (and monitored their mail after their accounts
were closed).  Last week, Dr. Georgiou sent mail to the system
administrators of Brown U., American U., and Univ. of Georgia (the 
three nodes of the List) claiming that he was suing the schools for
allowing us to "libel, slander, defame his character" and cause him
"great emotional stress, and problems with his family and friends."
The result being that the three schools decided within a day to close
this list down completely.  Luckily, we acted fast, and explained the
situation to them, so that 2/3 nodes relented.  

For years, the Turks and Skopjians (historically ethnic "enemies" of 
Greece) have tried to shut down our List with such methods as
sending fake mails and complaints to the sysadmins, thereby creating
problems for them, leading to general frustration with the administration
of our discussion List.  Then, in one day, one of our supposed "own" 
does it on his own, with one nasty, false mail.

A group of us are in the process of writing letters to administrators,
newspapers, and the like, but isn't it awful to think about how
much damage can be done, without even a fair chance at explanation?
As for the result, that remains to be seen.

That, at least, is one story for you, pleb...

