Yes, Canada is a quite liberal (in the free expression context) country
by global standards, one of the more so in the world, but it really is
2nd to the USA in this case, for two reasons.

The U.S. prohibition on censorship is much more explicit.  On the other
hand, the U.S. courts feel fairly free to apply fairly stretching
interpretations of it.  The Canadian courts have the explicit right to
do so without stretching.  The U.S. courts have a tougher time, but still
do it.

More important, however, is the attitude of the people.  When I was banned
at UW, most people were behind me, but not all.  And the Provost and
President were quite against me, particularly the provost.  And the
newspaper was against me as well.

At Stanford (which I live near now, but didn't then) there was more
support among the people, and in fact the administrators, when they
realized they had made a bonehead, took time only in finding a way of
undoing it while saving face -- at least in contrast to Alan George
at UW, who fought tooth and nail before giving up the fight.

Alan George (Provost at UW), oddly enough, is a Stanford grad, who
swore to me that Stanford would think just as he thought.
