Sexual harassment occurs on networks

By Andrea Lynn

In the faceless, often nameless world of computer networking, women - 
especially on college campuses - are being sexually harassed, say the 
contributors to a new book.

Often the harassers are strangers getting their kicks anonymously. The 
practice is doubly disturbing because it discourages women, who aren't 
wired into the world of high-technology to the degree that men are, from 
conversing in cyberspace - the realm of computers and other information 
systems.
 
"The problem is being reported by many women at many sites," Cheris 
Kramarae and H. Jeanie Taylor write of the obscenities, racial slurs and 
vicious personal attacks being sent to women.

"Women who want to be respected scholars will be very hesitant about 
publicly pointing to sexist behavior," write Kramarae and Taylor. "This 
silencing has serious consequences for interaction on individual nets, for
disciplines and for the academy in general."

Kramarae and Taylor, along with Maureen Ebben, are editors of and 
contributors to "Women, Information Technology, and Scholarship (WITS)," 
published in May by the UI Center for Advanced Study. At the UI, Kramarae
is the Jubilee Professor of Liberal Arts and Sciences and professor of 
speech communication, sociology, linguistics and women's studies; Taylor,
associate director of the Center for Advanced Study; and Ebben, a doctoral
candidate in speech communication. 

The book, which focuses on the social and political aspects of new 
information technologies, including computer networks, electronic mail, 
computer data bases, electronic publishing and distribution systems, 
includes articles by women in the forefront of the technology debate; and
an annotated bibliography of women and technology materials.

The book also contains digests of WITS Colloquium presentations. The 
colloquium, a group of UI faculty and academic professionals, seeks to 
ensure that "new communications technologies will be structured and used 
in ways that are beneficial and equitable for all." 

According to the authors, electronic bulletin boards have the most 
potential for abusive behavior. In the United States, there are more than
60,000 public access bulletin boards and 10 million people who use them 
regularly. 

There are, in fact, several problems associated with these boards. For 
instance, "men monopolize the talk," Kramarae and Taylor write. "Even open
networks where the topic is women's issues are, we have found, overrun by
men." Also, an increase in "high-tech titillation" can be expected, the 
authors say. "Many of us have received unrequested body-parts 'visuals' -
for example, 'breasts' composed of computer-keyboard letters and 
punctuation marks. 

"Our task is clear," write Kramarae and Ebben. "We need to create, 
electronically, a cyberspace of our own that fosters women's communication
and scholarship." 
