Consider a university that first gets email for some small group of
people for university research and business operations.  It is
normal for it to provide the service to those groups that will
best advance its goals.

Now suppose that later it provides email service and newsgroup service
to its entire community out of its own funds.  Suppose it says email
is a privilege and not a right, and we can withdraw it or restrict it
in any way we think will serve the best interests of the
university.  Whether this is legal or not will depend on whether it
is public or private and the precise state of law.

Whether it is legal or not, it is a bad idea to discriminate in
the provision of the service any more than it discriminates in
the more traditional services that it provides.
