John Wojdylo takes issue with Graham Stoney's remark that net/email
use is a privilege not a right and suggests that Stoney is *advocating*
that administrators flick switches for undue cause.  Well, that's
not how I read Graham's post; what I thought he was pointing out was
that universities provide our net/email support at their own cost,
and if they decided we weren't doing anything useful with it (let
alone if they thought we were doing something harmful with it) there'd
be nothing to stop them flicking a switch.  Explanations about freedom
of expression and why it was taken away?  Can you spell "BUDGET"?

I thought you could!

Quite apart from the fact that there is theoretically speaking no such
thing as a "right" (and yes, I'd be on the barricades for some anyway),
it's very hard to argue that some individual or institution has the
*obligation* to provide things to others (as opposed to doing them
harm) unless the things in question define that institution; and even
then . . . heard of uninsured people being turned away from hospitals
("public" hospitals too) in the U.S.?  Certainly we should be working
to make the net seem/be an essential part of how universities operate.
Meanwhile, let's not grandstand about "rights" that couldn't even have
existed a few years ago.

BTW this is a slightly different problem from the one John McCarthy
(I think) was discussing re Waterloo and Stanford; deletion of one
newsgroup while others are provided does beg the question of freedom
of information.  (But only deletion: there are plenty of groups not
every institution on the net has access to.)


